\details{
\verb{Data Index} holds one 16-byte record per scan: the scan's byte offset
into \verb{MS Raw Data} (\code{uint64}), the cycle number and a scan counter. Each
scan in \verb{MS Raw Data} is a 256-byte header followed by a run-length-encoded
profile. The header fields read here are:\tabular{lll}{
   \strong{Offset} \tab \strong{Type} \tab \strong{Field} \cr
   0–3 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Scan index (0-based) \cr
   4–7 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Cycle number \cr
   8–11 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Start of the cycle (milliseconds), as in \verb{Retention Time} \cr
   12–15 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Acquisition time of the scan (milliseconds) \cr
   16–19 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Acquisition event (0-based) \cr
   20–23 \tab \code{uint32} \tab MS level in the upper 16 bits, polarity in the lower 16 (0 = positive, 1 = negative) \cr
   144–147 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Last flight-time bin of the scan range \cr
   148–151 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Flight time of bin 0 \cr
   154–155 \tab \code{uint16} \tab Intensity multiplier \cr
   164–167 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Length of the profile in 16-bit words \cr
   168–171 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Total ion current \cr
   176–179 \tab \code{uint32} \tab Precursor m/z x 10000 (MS2 and above) \cr
}


\strong{Profile.} The profile is a sequence of 16-bit words. A word with bit 15
clear is a gap: the number of empty flight-time bins to skip. A word with
bit 15 set opens a run, and its lower 15 bits give the number of
intensities that follow, one per consecutive bin; the intensities
themselves use all 16 bits. A run of length 0 ends the scan.

\strong{Intensities.} The header's total ion current, and the \verb{TIC Data}
streams, equal the sum of the stored values times the scan's intensity
multiplier, so \code{scale = TRUE} multiplies them by it. The multiplier is
usually 128, but reaches 4096 in some intense scans of PXD025121.
'LabSolutions' displays and exports the profile
after a 5-point moving average, which is not applied here.

\strong{Mass axis.} Bin \code{b} of a scan has flight time \code{t = b + t0}, where \code{t0}
is read from the header. \verb{TTFL Tuning/Tuning Result 00} stores, for each
polarity, the reference masses of the calibrant (sodium trifluoroacetate
clusters) with their measured flight times. Between two reference ions,
\code{sqrt(mz)} is the quadratic in \code{t} through that pair and the next ion up;
below the first, it is the line through the first two. The last interval
and anything above it reuse the quadratic through the last three ions.

Checked against the vendor's own \code{TTFLDataExport} profile export of an
MTBLS688 file, this reproduces every intensity of all 3002 scans exactly,
once the 5-point moving average is applied, and every m/z to the four
decimal places the export keeps. The centroids of an MTBLS432 file
(vendor ANDI-MS exports) and of a PXD020792 file (Mascot Distiller peak
lists) agree to within a fraction of a flight-time bin. Above the last
reference ion, the median error against the Mascot Distiller centroids of
PXD025121 files stays within 2 ppm up to m/z 1500.
